Feature: The master checkout's main ref reflects what QA landed

  # BL-891 local-main-reflects-landed-01
  Scenario: A landed commit reaches the master checkout's main ref
    Given a master checkout on "main" with no local-only commits
    And QA has landed an approved commit by pushing its branch to the remote "main"
    When the landing completes
    Then the master checkout's "main" ref is the landed commit
    And the master checkout's working tree contains the landed files

  # BL-891 local-main-reflects-landed-02
  Scenario: Local-only bookkeeping commits are reconciled, not discarded
    Given a master checkout on "main" carrying local-only backlog bookkeeping commits
    And QA has landed an approved commit by pushing its branch to the remote "main"
    When the landing completes
    Then the landed commit is an ancestor of the master checkout's "main"
    And every local-only bookkeeping commit is still reachable from "main"

  # BL-891 local-main-reflects-landed-03
  Scenario: A master checkout that cannot be reconciled is surfaced, never forced
    Given a master checkout on "main" whose working tree has uncommitted changes
    And QA has landed an approved commit by pushing its branch to the remote "main"
    When the landing completes
    Then the master checkout's "main" ref is left untouched
    And the divergence is surfaced to the coordinator with its reason
    And no reset, rebase, stash or force-update is performed on the master checkout

  # BL-891 local-main-reflects-landed-04
  Scenario: The drift between the two refs is observable before it accumulates
    Given a master checkout on "main" that is behind the remote "main"
    When the drift check runs
    Then it reports how many commits "main" is behind the remote
    And it reports how many commits "main" is ahead of the remote
